# Credentials for the BROKERED providers (Example A — deploy/oauth/README.md).
#
# Copy to `.env.oauth` at the REPO ROOT (gitignored) and fill in. Consumed by
# scripts/keycloak-brokers.sh, which configures these as identity providers on the local Keycloak
# realm — Keycloak stays the token issuer, so go-oikumenea needs no config change.
#
#   set -a; . ./.env.oauth; set +a
#   scripts/keycloak-brokers.sh
#
# A provider is configured IFF its pair is set here; leave the rest commented out.
# Each provider's OAuth app must list this redirect URI:
#   http://localhost:8080/realms/oikumenea/broker/<alias>/endpoint
#
# For the DIRECT topology (Example B) the console reads AUTH_*-prefixed variables from
# web/.env.local instead — see web/.env.example.

# --- Google — console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth client (Web)
# OAUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
# OAUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""

# --- GitHub — github.com/settings/developers → New OAuth App
# The ONLY route by which GitHub can reach oikumenea: it is OAuth2 without OIDC, so it can never be a
# direct issuer. Needs the `user:email` scope (the script requests it) because primary addresses are
# often private.
# OAUTH_GITHUB_ID=""
# OAUTH_GITHUB_SECRET=""

# --- GitLab — gitlab.com → Settings → Applications (scopes: openid email profile)
# OAUTH_GITLAB_ID=""
# OAUTH_GITLAB_SECRET=""

# --- Microsoft Entra ID — portal.azure.com → App registrations
# The issuer is TENANT-scoped and MUST end in /v2.0.
# OAUTH_ENTRA_ID=""
# OAUTH_ENTRA_SECRET=""
# OAUTH_ENTRA_ISSUER="https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0"

# --- Okta (or an Auth0-shaped tenant) — admin console → Applications → Web
# Use the custom authorization server's issuer if you have one.
# OAUTH_OKTA_ID=""
# OAUTH_OKTA_SECRET=""
# OAUTH_OKTA_ISSUER="https://<tenant>.okta.com"
